The Security Council,

Recalling all previous resolutions and Presidential statements concerning the situation in Abyei and along the border between Sudan and South Sudan, and underlining the importance of full compliance with and implementation of these,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Sudan and South Sudan, and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations, and recalling the importance of the principles of good-neighbourliness, non-interference and regional cooperation,

Reiterating that the territorial boundaries of States shall not be altered by force, and that any territorial disputes shall be settled exclusively by peaceful means, affirming the priority it attaches to the full and urgent implementation of all outstanding issues from the Comprehensive Peace Agreement (CPA), underscoring that the future status of Abyei shall be resolved by negotiations between the parties in a manner consistent with the CPA and not by unilateral actions of either party, expressing grave concern that the South Sudan People’s Defence Forces (SSPDF) and other armed elements continue to operate in Abyei contrary to the Agreement on Temporary Arrangements for the Administration and Security of the Abyei Area of June 2011 and relevant resolutions that Abyei remain a demilitarized and weapons-free zone, and recalling prior agreements on the administration and security of the Abyei Area,

Encouraging the African Union, the African Union High-Level Implementation Panel, and the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for the Horn of Africa to intensify their mediation roles with Sudan and South Sudan to encourage them to establish temporary administrative and security arrangements for Abyei and to achieve a political solution for the status of Abyei,

Acknowledging that over the course of the fourteen years since the establishment of the United Nations Interim Security Force for Abyei (UNISFA), the mission has contributed towards the stabilization and demilitarization of the Abyei Area and together with the Joint Border Verification and Monitoring Mechanism (JBVMM) played a stabilizing role along the borders between South Sudan and Sudan,

Condemning the ongoing conflict in Sudan, including grave violations of international humanitarian law and violations and abuses of human rights, further expressing grave concern over the adverse impacts of the conflict on the Abyei Area, including the influx of displaced persons, the obstruction of engagement between Sudan and South Sudan to resolve the area’s political status, and the disruption of the JBVMM’s aerial monitoring operations due to ongoing hostilities and airspace restrictions,

Underlining that outbreaks of violence and proliferation of illicit arms by armed elements in the Abyei Area continue to pose a serious threat to the security situation and civilians, urging an immediate cessation of violence and respect for international humanitarian law, including obligations to allow and facilitate full, safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access to all in need, stressing in this regard the importance of UNISFA initiatives to support community dialogue and reconciliation efforts between the Misseriya and Ngok Dinka, as well as between the Ngok Dinka and Twic Dinka, and all other communities, and emphasizing the necessity of the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women and youth in these processes,

Condemning all attacks targeting civilians, humanitarian actors, United Nations personnel and peacekeepers in the Abyei Area, reiterating that attacks against peacekeepers may constitute war crimes and that those responsible must be held to account, expressing grave concern over ongoing threats to the safety and security of UNISFA personnel, and welcoming the willingness of local communities to pursue peace through dialogue,

Stressing that the Sudanese and South Sudanese authorities have the primary responsibility to ensure the safety, security and freedom of movement of United Nations personnel and assets per their obligations under the respective Status of Forces Agreements, and urging South Sudan to facilitate the full redeployment of UNISFA personnel to their previous locations, including in Gok Machar,

Expressing serious concern over the continued lack of progress by Sudan and South Sudan in establishing joint institutions in Abyei, including the Abyei Police Service, and emphasizing that the absence of institutions, rule of law, and essential services exacerbates instability and intercommunal tension,

Expressing deep concern over persistent delays by Sudan in the issuance of visas to United Nations police and corrections officers, which critically impairs UNISFA’s ability to execute its mandate to maintain security and support the rule of law, and urging Sudan to promptly issue all pending visas without conditions,

Expressing great concern at the deteriorating humanitarian situation in the Abyei Area, compounded by severe flooding and the adverse impacts of climate-related shocks, which exacerbate resource scarcity and displacement, and commending the dedication of UNISFA and humanitarian agencies in providing life-saving assistance,

Recognizing that the current situation in Abyei and along the border between Sudan and South Sudan continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

1. Decides to extend until 15 November 2026 the mandate of UNISFA as set out in paragraph 2 of resolution 1990 (2011) and, acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations, further decides to extend until 15 November 2026 the tasks of UNISFA as set out in paragraph 3 of resolution 1990 (2011);

2. Decides to extend until 15 November 2026 UNISFA’s mandate modification set forth in resolution 2024 (2011) and paragraph 1 of resolution 2075 (2012), which provides for UNISFA’s support to the Joint Border Verification and Monitoring Mechanism (JBVMM), and further decides that UNISFA shall continue to implement that mandate and tasks in accordance with resolution 2550 (2020) and this resolution;

3. Maintains the authorized troop ceiling at 3,250 and police ceiling at 640 police personnel, including 148 individual police officers and three formed police units, and expresses its intention to keep these ceilings under regular review in light of the security situation and recommendations of the Secretary-General;

4. Urges the Government of Sudan to facilitate the prompt and unhindered deployment of the authorized United Nations police personnel, including by swiftly issuing visas for all UNISFA police and civilian personnel without administrative delays, and reminds both Sudan and South Sudan of their obligations under their respective Status of Forces Agreements to guarantee full freedom of movement for all UNISFA personnel, equipment and supplies;

5. Reiterates that the Abyei Area shall be completely demilitarized from any armed forces, including the SSPDF and armed elements of local communities, other than UNISFA and the Abyei Police Service once established, and calls upon the Government of South Sudan to withdraw all SSPDF personnel and South Sudan security elements from southern Abyei immediately and without preconditions;

6. Urges both parties to immediately resume political dialogue on the final status of Abyei and temporary administrative and security arrangements as soon as security conditions permit, and calls upon both Governments to take constructive steps to establish the Abyei Joint Oversight Committee and the Abyei Police Service;

7. Calls upon the authorities in Sudan and South Sudan, as well as local leaders, to support UNISFA’s community-level conflict prevention and mediation efforts, to promote peaceful seasonal transhumance, and to de-escalate tensions, including between the Ngok Dinka and Twic Dinka communities, and encourages the full, equal and meaningful participation of women and youth in all peacebuilding and conflict mitigation mechanisms;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to inform the Security Council of progress in implementing UNISFA’s mandate, through written reports submitted every 180 days, with the first report no later than 1 May 2026 and the second no later than 15 October 2026;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

